  "text": "To find the sum of all integer bases \\( b > 9 \\) for which \\( 17_b \\) is a divisor of \\( 97_b \\), we need to convert these numbers to base 10 and then find the bases where the divisibility condition holds.\n\nFirst, convert \\( 17_b \\) and \\( 97_b \\) to base 10:\n- \\( 17_b = 1 \\cdot b + 7 = b + 7 \\)\n- \\( 97_b = 9 \\cdot b + 7 = 9b + 7 \\)\n\nFor \\( 17_b \\) to be a divisor of \\( 97_b \\), the following must hold:\n\\[ 9b + 7 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{b+7} \\]\n\nThis congruence means that \\( b+7 \\) must divide \\( 9b + 7 \\). Let's rewrite the congruence:\n\\[ 9b + 7 = k(b + 7) \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Rearrange to solve for \\( b \\):\n\\[ 9b + 7 = kb + 7k \\]\n\\[ 9b - kb = 7k - 7 \\]\n\\[ b(9 - k) = 7(k - 1) \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{7(k - 1)}{9 - k} \\]\n\nFor \\( b \\) to be an integer, \\( 9 - k \\) must divide \\( 7(k - 1) \\). Let's find the possible values of \\( k \\) that make \\( b \\) an integer and \\( b > 9 \\).\n\nWe need to check the values of \\( k \\) that make \\( b \\) an integer:\n1. \\( k = 0 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(0 - 1)}{9 - 0} = \\frac{-7}{9} \\] (not an integer)\n2. \\( k = 1 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(1 - 1)}{9 - 1} = \\frac{0}{8} = 0 \\] (not an integer)\n3. \\( k = 2 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(2 - 1)}{9 - 2} = \\frac{7}{7} = 1 \\] (not an integer)\n4. \\( k = 3 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(3 - 1)}{9 - 3} = \\frac{14}{6} = \\frac{7}{3} \\] (not an integer)\n5. \\( k = 4 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(4 - 1)}{9 - 4} = \\frac{21}{5} \\] (not an integer)\n6. \\( k = 5 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(5 - 1)}{9 - 5} = \\frac{28}{4} = 7 \\] (not an integer)\n7. \\( k = 6 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(6 - 1)}{9 - 6} = \\frac{35}{3} \\] (not an integer)\n8. \\( k = 7 \\):\n   \\[ b = \\frac{7(7 - 1)}{9 - 7} = \\frac{42}{2} = 21 \\] (an integer)\n\nSo, the only valid base is \\( b = 21 \\). Therefore, the sum of all such bases is:\n\\[ \\boxed{21} \\]",
